      ],
      "modes": [
        {
          "id": "carbonate_stretch_asymmetric_monodentate",
          "label": "νₐₛ(OCO)",
          "topology": "monodentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₁",
          "symmetry": "A′",
          "wn_start": 1450,
          "wn_end": 1490,
          "note": "One terminal (non-anchored) oxygen pulls in toward the carbon while the other pushes away — the two surface-bound complex's terminal C-O bonds stretching out of phase — and the carbon itself leans slightly toward the oxygen that's moving in toward it. The single anchor oxygen (Oₛ) stays put. This large, asymmetric dipole change makes it typically the highest-frequency, most intense band in the spectrum — the highest of all six A′/A″ modes, so it's ν₁ under the standard decreasing-frequency-within-species convention (see the Notation box). Physical picture and wavenumber from Solis et al.'s Fig. 5 (their own figure numbers these modes differently — see the Notation box for why this page doesn't follow that). ν(C-O), A′, 1450–1490 cm⁻¹.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": ["site-sensitive"],
          "reference": ["Solis.InitialStagesCO2.2017"]
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_stretch_symmetric_monodentate",
          "label": "νₛ(OCO)",
          "topology": "monodentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₂",
          "symmetry": "A′",
          "wn_start": 1350,
          "wn_end": 1370,
          "note": "Both terminal oxygens stretch outward and inward together, in phase, while the carbon itself dips slightly toward the anchor oxygen (Oₛ) — which otherwise stays put. Second-highest of the five A′ modes, hence ν₂. Physical picture and wavenumber from Solis et al.'s Fig. 5 (see νₐₛ(OCO)'s note for why this page's index doesn't match their own figure). ν(C-O)+ν(C-Oₛ), A′, 1350–1370 cm⁻¹.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": ["site-sensitive"],
          "reference": ["Solis.InitialStagesCO2.2017"]
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_stretch_anchor_monodentate",
          "label": "ν(C-O)",
          "category": "stretch",
          "atoms": "C-O",
          "topology": "monodentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₃",
          "symmetry": "A′",
          "wn_start": 1050,
          "wn_end": 1070,
          "note": "The free ion's true totally-symmetric stretch, ν₁ of D₃h — IR-inactive there, but resolved and IR-active once surface binding breaks that symmetry; the one stretch with no sym/asym partner, since it singles out whichever one oxygen ISN'T equivalent to the other two. For monodentate coordination the odd one out is the one surface-anchored oxygen, so this is mainly the C-Oₛ single-bond stretch to it — but the coupled ν(C-Oₛ)+ν(C-O) character in the label shows up as the two terminal oxygens also leaning slightly toward the carbon as it moves. Third-highest of the five A′ modes, hence ν₃. Physical picture and wavenumber from Solis et al.'s Fig. 5 (see νₐₛ(OCO)'s note for why this page's index doesn't match their own figure). ν(C-Oₛ)+ν(C-O), A′, 1050–1070 cm⁻¹.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": [],
          "reference": ["Solis.InitialStagesCO2.2017"]
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_wagging_monodentate",
          "label": "γ(OCO)",
          "subtype": "wagging",
          "topology": "monodentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₆",
          "symmetry": "A″",
          "wn_start": 850,
          "wn_end": 860,
          "note": "Both terminal oxygens push straight out of the molecular plane together while the central carbon buckles back the opposite way — the only out-of-plane mode of the six, which is why it alone carries the antisymmetric double-prime (A″); every other mode here stays flat in-plane (A′). The anchor oxygen (Oₛ), which the plane itself is defined relative to, doesn't move. As the lone non-totally-symmetric mode, it's numbered last (ν₆) under the standard convention, even though its own frequency falls in the middle of the other five — see the Notation box. Physical picture and wavenumber from Solis et al.'s Fig. 5 (their own figure numbers this mode ν₃ instead — see νₐₛ(OCO)'s note). π, A″, 850–860 cm⁻¹.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": [],
          "reference": ["Solis.InitialStagesCO2.2017"]
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_scissoring_monodentate",
          "label": "δ(OCO)",
          "subtype": "scissoring",
          "topology": "monodentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₄",
          "symmetry": "A′",
          "wn_start": 740,
          "wn_end": 780,
          "note": "The two terminal oxygens open and close their shared angle relative to each other, like a pair of scissors, while the carbon dips slightly toward the anchor oxygen (Oₛ) at the same time — the anchor oxygen itself stays put either way. Fourth-highest of the five A′ modes, hence ν₄. Physical picture and wavenumber from Solis et al.'s Fig. 5 (see νₐₛ(OCO)'s note for why this page's index doesn't match their own figure). δ(OCOₛ), A′, 740–780 cm⁻¹.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": [],
          "reference": ["Solis.InitialStagesCO2.2017"]
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_rocking_monodentate",
          "label": "ρ(OCO)",
          "category": "bend",
          "subtype": "rocking",
          "atoms": "O-C-O",
          "topology": "monodentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₅",
          "symmetry": "A′",
          "wn_start": 680,
          "wn_end": 700,
          "note": "A seesaw about the fixed anchor oxygen (Oₛ): the carbon tilts one way while both terminal oxygens swing slightly the OPPOSITE way, rather than changing their own mutual angle the way scissoring above does. The lowest-frequency of the five A′ modes, hence ν₅ (last among A′, just ahead of the A″ wagging mode below). Physical picture and wavenumber from Solis et al.'s Fig. 5 (see νₐₛ(OCO)'s note for why this page's index doesn't match their own figure). δ(OₛCO), A′, 680–700 cm⁻¹.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": [],
          "reference": ["Solis.InitialStagesCO2.2017"]
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_stretch_anchor_bidentate",
          "label": "ν(C-O)",
          "category": "stretch",
          "atoms": "C-O",
          "topology": "bidentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₁",
          "symmetry": "A₁",
          "wn_start": 1600,
          "wn_end": 1650,
          "note": "The free ion's true totally-symmetric stretch, ν₁ of D₃h — IR-inactive there, but resolved and IR-active once surface binding breaks that symmetry; the one stretch with no sym/asym partner, since it singles out whichever one oxygen ISN'T equivalent to the other two. For bidentate coordination the odd one out is the free, non-coordinated oxygen, so this is that oxygen's own terminal C=Oₜ stretch, pushing and pulling along its own bond axis — with the carbon itself recoiling slightly the opposite way. A₁, 1600–1650 cm⁻¹, the highest-frequency bidentate mode, well clear of the ring-stretch pair below.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": [],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_stretch_asymmetric_bidentate",
          "label": "νₐₛ(OCO)",
          "topology": "bidentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₄",
          "symmetry": "B₂",
          "wn_start": 1250,
          "wn_end": 1320,
          "note": "Two of the three C-O bonds stretch oppositely (one lengthens, one shortens) while the third stays put — one component of the doubly-degenerate asymmetric stretch, ν₃ of the free CO₃²⁻ ion (D₃h); surface binding splits that degenerate pair and lowers the overall symmetry to C₂ᵥ for bidentate coordination. The two surface-bound C-Oᵦ bonds engage in this out-of-phase tug-of-war — one stretches as the other compresses — with the free oxygen leaning slightly the opposite way. B₂, 1250–1320 cm⁻¹.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": ["site-sensitive"],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_stretch_symmetric_bidentate",
          "label": "νₛ(OCO)",
          "topology": "bidentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₂",
          "symmetry": "A₁",
          "wn_start": 1000,
          "wn_end": 1070,
          "note": "The two non-surface-bound C-O bonds stretch in phase — the other resolved component of the same doubly-degenerate ν₃ pair (paired with νₐₛ(OCO) above). Both surface-pinned oxygens expand and contract their C-Oᵦ bonds in absolute unison; the free oxygen drifts slightly the opposite way as a small counter-motion. A₁, 1000–1070 cm⁻¹, symmetric stretch of the two coordinated oxygens.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": ["site-sensitive"],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_wagging_bidentate",
          "label": "γ(OCO)",
          "subtype": "wagging",
          "topology": "bidentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₆",
          "symmetry": "B₁",
          "wn_start": 830,
          "wn_end": 850,
          "note": "The central C buckles out of the O-C-O plane toward the viewer while the free oxygen recoils the opposite way, back into the page — the out-of-plane bend, ν₂ of the free ion, non-degenerate so it doesn't split into two distinct motions on binding (though its frequency still shifts a little by topology). Both surface-pinned oxygens, which anchor the plane itself, stay fixed. B₁, 830–850 cm⁻¹ — the one mode whose out-of-plane motion actually flips the coordinate signs across the molecular plane, unlike every A₁/B₂ mode here.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": [],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_scissoring_bidentate",
          "label": "δ(OCO)",
          "subtype": "scissoring",
          "topology": "bidentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₃",
          "symmetry": "A₁",
          "wn_start": 750,
          "wn_end": 800,
          "note": "Ring flattening: the inner Oᵦ-C-Oᵦ bound angles open and pinch symmetrically like a pair of scissors, entirely in-plane — shown here as the carbon and the free oxygen drawing apart from each other symmetrically, since the two surface-pinned oxygens that define the ring can't themselves move. One component of the doubly-degenerate in-plane bend, ν₄ of the free ion. A₁, 750–800 cm⁻¹.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": [],
          "reference": []
        },
        {
          "id": "carbonate_rocking_bidentate",
          "label": "ρ(OCO)",
          "category": "bend",
          "subtype": "rocking",
          "atoms": "O-C-O",
          "topology": "bidentate",
          "herzberg_notation": "ν₅",
          "symmetry": "B₂",
          "wn_start": 550,
          "note": "The other resolved component of the same doubly-degenerate in-plane bend as δ(OCO) above — here the carbon and the free oxygen tilt left and right together, rhythmically, rather than swinging toward/apart from each other; the two surface-pinned oxygens, the fixed pivot this tilts relative to, don't move. B₂, below ~550 cm⁻¹ — the lowest-frequency mode of the six, often outside the practical DRIFTS detection window.",
          "ir_active": true,
          "raman_active": true,
          "tags": [],
          "reference": []
        }
      ]
    },
